Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be complex numbers such that $|\beta| = 1$ and $\overline{\alpha} \beta \neq 1.$  Find the maximum value of
\[\left| \frac{\beta - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta} \right|.\right>
<answer>Following is a solution.
Make the substitution $\beta = e^{i\theta}, |\theta| < \pi.$  Then
\begin{align*}
\frac{\beta - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta}
&= \frac{e^{i\theta} - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} e^{i\theta}} \\
&= \frac{(e^{i\theta} - \alpha)(1 - \alpha e^{-i\theta})}{(1 - |\alpha|^2) e^{-i\theta}} \\
\left| \frac{\beta - \alpha}{1 - \overline{\alpha} \beta} \right|
&= \frac{1}{|1 - \alpha \overline{\alpha}|}
     \left| \frac{(e^{i\theta} - \alpha)(e^{-i\theta} - \alpha)}{1} \right|
\le \frac{1}{|1 - |\alpha|^2|}
     \left| \frac{|e^{i\theta}|^2 + |\alpha|^2 - 2\mathrm{Re}(\alpha e^{i\theta})}{1} \right| \\
&= \frac{1}{1 - |\alpha|^2}
     \left| 1 + |\alpha|^2 - 2\mathrm{Re}(\alpha e^{i\theta}) \right| \\
&\le \frac{1}{1 - |\alpha|^2}
     \left| 1 + |\alpha|^2 - 2|\alpha^2| \right| \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1 + |\alpha|^2}{-|\alpha|^2 + |\alpha| + 1}}.
\end{align*}
We recognize that $f(e^{i\theta})$ achieves its maximum over $\theta \in (-\pi, \pi)$ at $\theta = 0$ if and only if $f(z)$ is a decreasing function of
$|z| = |e^{i\theta}| = 1$.  We first show that $g(e^{i\theta})$ is an increasing function of $|e^{i\theta}| = 1$:
\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{d|e^{i\theta}|} g(e^{i\theta})
&= \frac{d}{d|e^{i\theta}|}
     \left| f(\hat{\alpha}) \right| \\
&= 0
\end{align*}
if and only if
\[\frac{\hat{\alpha}}{1 - \overline{\alpha}\hat{\alpha}} = k
     \sqrt{|e^{i\theta}|^2 + |\alpha|^2 - 2\mathrm{Re}(\alpha e^{i\theta})}\]
for some $k \in \mathbb{C}$.  If $\hat{\alpha} = 0$, this equation holds for all $|e^{i\theta}| \leq 1$.  Thus the maximum value of $|f(e^{i\theta})|$ occurs at $\theta = 0$. $\square$</answer>

